Patches to OpenAL Soft 1.19.1 (applied by build/build-openal.sh, patch -p1)

openal-soft-1.19.1-riscos.patch
    Two build fixes, neither of which changes what the library does, NEON
    detection on RISC OS, and a RISC OS way to set the period size:
    - Alc/bformatdec.h: N3D2N3DScale, SN3D2N3DScale and FuMa2N3DScale
      declared extern. They were tentative definitions in the header, one
      per file that includes it, which GCC 10 (-fno-common by default)
      rejects at link time. Later OpenAL Soft releases fixed it the same
      way.
    - Alc/helpers.c SetRTPriority(): the real-time priority code is left
      out on __riscos__. UnixLib has pthread_setschedparam but no
      sched_get_priority_min (and no real-time scheduling). Only the ALSA,
      OSS, sndio, Solaris and WASAPI backends call it, none of which are
      built; the SDL2 backend doesn't.
    - Alc/helpers.c FillCPUCaps(): on __riscos__ NEON is detected with
      VFPSupport_Features (MVFR1's Advanced SIMD load/store, integer and
      single precision fields all 1), not /proc/cpuinfo, which RISC OS
      doesn't have. The system variable OpenAL$NoNEON (any value) keeps
      the C mixers. build/build-openal.sh builds the NEON mixers
      (-DALSOFT_CPUEXT_NEON=ON; only mixer_neon.c gets -mfpu=neon).
      Asked for by the ClassiCube port (2026-10-29).
    - Alc/mixvoice.c, Alc/ALu.c: with RO_NEON_EXACT_ONLY (which
      build/build-openal.sh sets) only the NEON mixers that give the same
      samples as the C ones bit for bit are used: Mix, MixRow and the
      linear resampler (OpenAL's default). The bsinc resamplers' NEON
      version differed in the last bit (float output: up to 2.4e-7; 16-bit
      output: 86 samples in 192000 off by 1), and the HRTF ones aren't
      checked, so those stay C. tests/host-harness/openal/neon.sh mixes a
      fixed scene with both and compares them.
    - Alc/ALc.c: on __riscos__ the system variable OpenAL$PeriodSize sets
      period_size (samples mixed at a time; with the SDL2 backend, also
      SDL's buffer size), so a program's !Run can set it without an
      alsoft.conf. It wins over the config file. How much sound is kept
      queued is set by SDL (SDL_RISCOS_AUDIO_QUEUE_MS), not here.

The two build fixes are taken from the Warzone 2100 port (riscos-warzone2100
patches/openal/openal-soft-1.19.1-riscos.diff), with comments added.

They are under OpenAL Soft's licence, the GNU LGPL v2 or later (see
LICENCES.txt).

Build notes (build/build-openal.sh has them as comments too):
- Only the SDL2 output backend (and Null) is built: on RISC OS the sound
  goes OpenAL -> SDL's audio thread -> the SDL overlay's RISC OS audio
  driver -> SharedSoundBuffer.
- UnixLib only declares nanosleep() and friends when asked for POSIX, and
  OpenAL builds with -std=c11: hence -D_POSIX_C_SOURCE=200809L
  -D_XOPEN_SOURCE=700.
- OpenAL builds two table generators (bin2h, bsincgen) for the build
  machine in a sub-build that inherits CC/CFLAGS from the environment, so
  make runs with those unset; the library's compiler comes from
  build/riscos.cmake.
- Configuration: OpenAL reads the file named by the ALSOFT_CONF variable
  (and alsoft.conf in the usual Unix places, which UnixLib maps to RISC OS
  paths). Errors are logged to stderr, which in a desktop program opens a
  command window: set ALSOFT_LOGFILE to a file to send them there.

Test: tests/host-harness/openal (the same source built for Linux, its
sound recorded and checked); tests/altest.c on RISC OS.
